 <u> SUMMARY </u> :  Extends funding for a specific school district.   
Specifically, <u> this bill </u> : 

1)Authorizes the Plumas Unified School District (PUSD) to be  
  eligible to continue to receive "necessary small schools"  
  funding by extending the June 30, 1999 sunset of such funding  
  that exists in current law until July 1, 2002, and, repeals it  
  as of January 1, 2003, unless a later enacted statute that is  
  enacted before January 1, 2003, deletes or extends the dates  
  on which it becomes inoperative and is repealed. 

2)Requires the district to report by July 1, 2001 to the State  
  Department of Education and Department of Finance on its plan  
  to address its shortfall in the future when this section is  
  repealed.
 <u> 
EXISTING LAW </u> :  

1)Provides additional funding to "necessary small schools" if  
  they reside in a district with fewer than 2,501 units of  
  average daily attendance (ADA).  A "necessary small school" is  
  defined as follows:  a) elementary schools with average ADA of  
  less than 101 and that meet other specifications; and, (b)  
  high schools with average ADA of less than 301.

2)Authorizes, pursuant to SB 1155 (Leslie), Chapter 865,  
  Statutes of 1997, PUSD to be eligible to receive "necessary  






                                                         <u> AB 1
                                                         </u> Page  2

  small schools" funding by:  a) specifying narrow criteria that  
  only this district meets; and, b) by increasing the ADA cutoff  
  for program eligibility from 2,501 ADA or less to 4,501 ADA.   
  Chapter 865 sunsets June 30, 1999.

 <u> FISCAL EFFECT </u> :  Continued General Fund (Proposition. 98) funding  
of approximately $660,000 to PUSD until July 1, 2002.

 <u> COMMENTS </u> :  PUSD argues that it faces unique circumstances which  
merit its inclusion among districts that receive necessary small  
schools funding.  First, PUSD serves an entire county that is  
geographically larger than Rhode Island or Delaware, a situation  
that leads to higher-than-average operations costs.  Second,  
PUSD had experienced a sharp drop in district revenues due to a  
recent downturn in the timber industry and the consequent drop  
in federal Forest Reserve funds.

The sunset and report was added to the funding in order to end  
the continued funding.
